// @korulang/unikraft-paging — Unikraft's page-table API (`ukpaging`), lifted.
//
//   import unikraft/paging
//
// WHAT THIS IS. `lib/ukpaging` is the layer BELOW `unikraft/vmem`: raw page
// tables, not address-space areas. `uk_vma_map` (ukvmem) calls down into
// `uk_paging_page_mapx` (this library) to actually write page-table entries.
// 15 exported functions, 140 `UK_ASSERT`s — the second-highest count of any
// library on the shelf — and eleven public-header `static inline`s: eight are
// pure arithmetic (FREE, case 3), three are inline assembly reading or writing
// a control register (case 3 NO — nothing to call, only something to re-emit).
//
// THE HEADLINE FINDING, AND WHY THIS SLOT WAS STILL OPEN. `uk_paging_pt_set_active`
// switches the CPU's live address space — the machinery the program is running
// on. Its own doc comment states the rule in English: "The code of the function
// must be mapped into the new address space at the same virtual address." NO
// `UK_ASSERT` anywhere in paging.c checks this. Nothing checks it at all. Get it
// wrong and the very next instruction fetch after the `mov %cr3` is unmapped —
// not a wrong answer, not an errno, an immediate triple fault, in debug and
// release builds alike. This is the SAME SHAPE as `unikraft/vmem`'s two headline
// rules (a fatal mistake the C cannot detect because it isn't a function call,
// it's a hardware state change) and it is worse, because the "access" that
// triggers it is unavoidable: it is the next instruction, whichever one that is.
//
// PROOF THE RULE IS REAL, FROM THE C'S OWN BOOTSTRAP. `uk_paging_init()`
// (paging.c:1478-1545) — the ONLY caller of `uk_paging_pt_init` anywhere in the
// tree (grepped; zero others) — does NOT call `pt_set_active` right after
// `pt_init`. It calls `uk_paging_page_map` once per boot memory region FIRST
// (mapping the kernel's own code, data, and boot structures into the fresh
// table) and activates the table LAST. A `pt_init`'d table is allocated via a
// brand-new, zeroed top-level page (`pg_pt_alloc`, paging.c:255-259) and maps
// nothing but the directmap window (if configured) until something explicitly
// maps into it — so activating it right after `pt_init`, skipping that mapping
// step, is exactly the fatal, unasserted program this header comment warns
// about, demonstrated by the one caller in the tree carefully avoiding it.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS LIFT'S TYPES. A `pt_init`'d table therefore mints
// `<init!>`, and NO tor in this module accepts `<!init>` as input to
// `pt_set_active`. The only way to reach a state `pt_set_active` accepts is
// `pt_clone` of the table THIS PROGRAM IS ALREADY RUNNING ON
// (`uk_paging_pt_clone`, not called anywhere else in the tree either — this
// lift's boot demo is the first exercise of it outside its own module). A clone
// (without `UK_PAGING_PAGE_FLAG_CLONE_NEW`) deep-copies every present PTE from
// the source, so it maps everything the currently-running code needs by
// construction, and switching to it is survivable. `pt_clone` mints
// `<active-safe!>`; only `<!active-safe>` satisfies `pt_set_active`.
//
// A SECOND FATAL, UNASSERTED RULE THIS LIFT FOUND BY READING, NOT BY GREPPING
// `UK_ASSERT`. `uk_paging_pt_free` (paging.c:321-341) never checks whether the
// table it is about to unmap and free is `pg_active_pt` — the table the CPU is
// currently running on. Freeing the live table would free the very page-table
// pages mapping the code that called `pt_free`. Nothing in the C stops this;
// there is no `UK_ASSERT(pt != pg_active_pt)` anywhere in the function. So
// `pt_free` here accepts `<!init|!active-safe|!detached>` and NEVER `<!active>`
// — the state `pt_set_active` mints is not one this module's `pt_free` will
// ever take back. See `tests/negative_free_active_table.kz`.
//
// A THIRD FINDING, A MISMATCH RATHER THAN A MISSING CHECK. "The active page
// table" is a machine-wide SINGLETON — one CR3 register — but this lift's
// obligations are per-HANDLE, the same shape `unikraft/store` found for
// refcounts: a COUNT (or here, a single hardware register) is not the same
// shape as a claim about ONE binding. Calling `pt_set_active` on a second,
// independent `<active-safe!>` handle mints `<active!>` on IT without doing
// anything to the FIRST handle's type, which stays `<active!>` even though at
// most one table is truly live at a time. This lift resolves it the same
// direction `store` did: never smoothed over, and conservative rather than
// wrong. A handle that WAS activated and has since been superseded by a later
// `pt_set_active` on a different handle can never be freed again through THIS
// lift — its type still claims `<active!>`, and `pt_free` still refuses it.
// That is a real cost (a safe program this lift will not compile) paid for a
// real guarantee (this lift will never let you free the table you are
// currently running on). See "Claims this lift does not make" in README.md.
//
// WHAT THIS LIFT DOES NOT ATTEMPT, STATED HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATE.
// `pt_init` IS lifted — it is phantom-typed, has a real Zig implementation that
// calls the real C function, and its fatal-if-activated-directly state is what
// `tests/negative_activate_bare_init.kz` proves refused. It is NOT exercised in
// the live boot demo. The reason: `pt_init(pt, start, len)` needs a physical
// address range that is GENUINELY FREE — not backing this program's own code,
// stack, heap, or any other live mapping. Discovering that honestly needs
// `ukplat_memregion_foreach` or the frame allocator `uk_paging_init` already
// owns, and NEITHER is on ukpaging's own 15-symbol surface. Handing `pt_init` a
// physical range this lift merely guesses is free — e.g. the backing of one of
// its own static buffers — would violate the exact contract the doc states
// ("the range must not be assigned to other page tables") in a way nothing
// would catch until the two allocators handed out the same physical frame
// twice. Reaching into `uk_falloc` or `ukplat_memregion` to solve it would also
// cross the brief's "bind at the native altitude" rule. So this lift ships
// `pt_init` honestly typed and honestly unexercised, rather than faking a boot
// with a physical range that was never provably free. `uk_paging_pt_add_mem`,
// `uk_paging_page_mapx`'s custom `mapx` callback, `uk_paging_page_set_attr`,
// and `uk_paging_init` itself are likewise not bound; see README.md.
//
// THE STRUCT MIRROR THIS LIFT DOES NOT NEED. `struct uk_pagetable` is a real,
// public, non-`#ifdef`-guarded-in-the-default-config struct (paging.h:316-327)
// — but unlike `unikraft/blk`, `unikraft/vmem`, `unikraft/lock`, this module
// never reads or writes one of its fields. Every accessor is a real exported C
// call (`pt_get_active`, `pt_walk`, ...). So no offset claim is needed, only an
// upper bound on the struct's SIZE, to allocate storage for a caller-owned
// instance the way `uk_paging_pt_init`/`_clone` require. `PT_STORAGE_BYTES`
// below is one page — three orders of magnitude more than the struct needs
// even with `CONFIG_LIBUKPAGING_STATS` on (3 scalars + 3 `unsigned long[5]`
// arrays, at most ~144 bytes) — so this lift makes NO precise-sizeof claim the
// way `unikraft/lock` had to correct one, because it does not need one.
//
// PAGE ATTRIBUTES AND THE PTE FORMAT ARE HARDCODED, NOT DERIVED — the same
// judgement call `unikraft/vmem` made for `PROT_READ`/`PROT_WRITE`, cited here
// rather than re-argued: x86_64 `plat/native/arch/x86_64/.../page.h:57-59` and
// `arch/x86_64.h:186` are ISA facts (the PTE present bit, the base page size),
// not Kconfig-chosen values, so there is nothing to probe for. A platform that
// renumbered them would be caught by a page fault, not a refusal.

const PageTable = opaque {};

// exportsyms.uk. Every symbol below is on it (case 1 — the allowlist is the
// full surface; 0 phantom, 0 inert, measured against
// `nm -g liblibukpaging.o` on a built image).
extern fn uk_paging_pt_get_active() ?*PageTable;
extern fn uk_paging_pt_set_active(pt: *PageTable) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_pt_init(pt: *PageTable, start: usize, len: usize) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_pt_clone(pt: *PageTable, pt_src: *PageTable, flags: c_ulong) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_pt_free(pt: *PageTable, flags: c_ulong) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_pt_walk(
    pt: *PageTable,
    vaddr: usize,
    level: *c_uint,
    pt_vaddr: ?*usize,
    pte: ?*u64,
) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_page_mapx(
    pt: *PageTable,
    vaddr: usize,
    paddr: usize,
    pages: c_ulong,
    attr: c_ulong,
    flags: c_ulong,
    mapx: ?*anyopaque,
) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_page_unmap(pt: *PageTable, vaddr: usize, pages: c_ulong, flags: c_ulong) c_int;
extern fn uk_paging_virt_to_phys(address: usize) usize;
extern fn uk_paging_page_kmap(pt: *PageTable, paddr: usize, pages: c_ulong, flags: c_ulong) usize;
extern fn uk_paging_page_kunmap(pt: *PageTable, vaddr: usize, pages: c_ulong, flags: c_ulong) void;

// nolibc, backed by ukalloc's default allocator. Same seam `unikraft/blk` and
// `unikraft/vmem` use for their own caller-owned handles.
extern fn malloc(size: usize) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn free(ptr: ?*anyopaque) void;

// --- Constants. All named-and-cited, none derived by probe; see the header
// note on why this module has no Kconfig-integer or VADDR_ANY problem to
// solve the way `unikraft/vmem` did.

// x86_64 `plat/native/arch/x86_64/include/uk/plat/native/arch/page.h:25-26`.
// The base page-table level's page size. An ISA fact on x86_64: level 0 is
// always 4 KiB. (Larger levels are Kconfig/CPU-feature-conditional; this lift
// only ever maps at level 0, so it never needs those.)
const PAGE_SIZE: usize = 0x1000;

// Same file, :57-58. Page protection bits — identical numbering to
// `unikraft/vmem`'s (page.h:56-59 there is the SAME file), cited rather than
// re-derived.
const PROT_READ: c_ulong = 0x01;
const PROT_WRITE: c_ulong = 0x02;
const PROT_RW: c_ulong = PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE;

// `arch/x86_64/include/uk/arch/x86_64.h:186`. The PTE "present" bit — again an
// ISA fact (the x86-64 page-table entry format), not a Kconfig value.
const PTE_PRESENT: u64 = 0x001;

// `UK_PAL_PADDR_INV` / `UK_PAGING_PADDR_ANY`: the sentinel `uk_paging_page_mapx`
// checks `paddr` against to decide "allocate a fresh frame for me" versus "map
// exactly this physical address" (paging.c:540-547). It is
// `UK_PLAT_NATIVE_PAGE_HUGE_ALIGN_DOWN(0xBAADBAADBAADBAAD)` — the IDENTICAL
// formula `unikraft/vmem` used for `UK_PAGING_VADDR_ANY`
// (`unikraft/vmem/index.kz:236-241`), because `addr.h` defines
// `UK_PLAT_NATIVE_VADDR_INV` and `UK_PLAT_NATIVE_PADDR_INV` with the SAME macro
// over the SAME seed. Reused by citation, not re-derived: `vmem`'s probe
// already proved the value is `0xbaadbaad80000000` on this platform, and nothing
// here has a second, independent way to ask. **A wrong guess here is not a
// theoretical risk — an earlier draft of this lift used `~0` (all-ones)
// instead of this constant.** `uk_paging_page_mapx` treated that as "map
// SCRATCH to the literal physical address 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF" rather than
// "allocate a frame for me" — `uk_pal_paddr_range_isvalid` would have refused
// it, but that check is a `UK_ASSERT` (paging.c:543) and compiles out. The
// program built and booted; `pt-walk`'s own PTE read reported the mapping
// PRESENT at an absurd physical address, and the very next real access
// crashed the machine. Found on the first live QEMU run, not by inspection —
// see README.md's toolchain-defects section for the literal console output
// and the fix.
const PADDR_INV: usize = 0xbaadbaad80000000;

// `arch/x86_64/include/uk/plat/native/arch/addr.h:44-45` /
// `plat/native/arch/x86_64/include/uk/plat/native/arch/pt.h:39-40`, combined:
// `UK_PLAT_NATIVE_PT_Lx_PTE_PADDR(pte, lvl)` is
// `((pte) & __X86_64_PTE_PADDR_MASK) & UK_PLAT_NATIVE_PAGE_MASK`, i.e. bits
// 12..51. CASE 3 FREE: pure arithmetic over a value this lift already has (a
// PTE `pt_walk` handed back), no symbol needed.
const PTE_PADDR_MASK: u64 = ((@as(u64, 1) << 52) - 1) & ~@as(u64, PAGE_SIZE - 1);

// `arch/x86_64/include/uk/plat/native/arch/addr.h`: `__X86_64_VADDR_BITS` is 48
// for the default (non-5-level) page table depth this lift assumes throughout
// (`UK_PAL_PT_LEVELS` defaults to 4). CASE 3 FREE reconstruction of
// `uk_pal_vaddr_isvalid`/`_range_isvalid` (addr.h's own body is pure sign-
// extension arithmetic, no symbol) — genuinely retires paging.c:390,538,972,
// 1256's `UK_ASSERT(uk_pal_vaddr_isvalid(...))` family: an address this check
// refuses is never handed to the C at all.
fn vaddrCanonical(addr: usize) bool {
    const shift: u6 = 64 - 48;
    const signed: i64 = @bitCast(addr);
    const rt = (signed << shift) >> shift;
    return @as(u64, @bitCast(rt)) == addr;
}

// UK_PAGING_PAGE_FLAG_CLONE_NEW (paging.h:349).
const CLONE_NEW: c_ulong = 0x01;

const EINVAL: i32 = -22;

// One page. Three orders of magnitude more than `struct uk_pagetable` needs
// even with `CONFIG_LIBUKPAGING_STATS` on; see the header note "the struct
// mirror this lift does not need."
const PT_STORAGE_BYTES: usize = 4096;

fn allocPt() ?*PageTable {
    const mem = malloc(PT_STORAGE_BYTES) orelse return null;
    return @ptrCast(mem);
}

// ============================================================================
// ACTIVE — the table this program is already running on
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_pt_get_active`, no phantom state at all. Deliberately BARE, like
// `unikraft/vmem`'s `*Space`: this handle is read-only for every operation
// this lift offers on it (`pt_clone`/`pt_clone.empty` never write through it),
// so there is nothing to ratchet and it can be cloned from any number of
// times. It is NOT assignable where `*PageTable<!active-safe>` or
// `*PageTable<!init|!active-safe|!detached>` is required — those need a real
// phantom state and a bare pointer does not have one, so misuse is a type
// error, not an obligation one.
~pub tor active { }
| ready *PageTable
| unavailable

~proc active|zig {
    const pt = uk_paging_pt_get_active() orelse {
        return .{ .unavailable = .{} };
    };
    return .{ .ready = pt };
}

// ============================================================================
// PT_CLONE — the only way this lift reaches a table `pt_set_active` accepts
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_pt_clone(new, source, 0)` — deep-copies every present PTE from
// `source` (paging.c:84-217), sharing `source`'s frame allocator
// (`pt_dst->fa = pt_src->fa`, paging.c:104), so it needs no physical-memory
// range of its own. Because it copies the WHOLE hierarchy, the result maps
// everything `source` maps — including, when `source` came from `active`, the
// code and data this program is running on right now. Mints <active-safe!>,
// the ONLY state `pt_set_active` accepts.
~pub tor pt-clone { source: *PageTable }
| ok *PageTable<active-safe!>
| refused { errno: i32, reason: string }

~proc pt-clone|zig {
    const dst = allocPt() orelse {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = -12, .reason = "out of memory allocating the destination page table" } };
    };
    const rc = uk_paging_pt_clone(dst, source, 0);
    if (rc != 0) {
        free(@ptrCast(dst));
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = @intCast(rc), .reason = "uk_paging_pt_clone could not copy the source hierarchy" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = dst };
}

// The other value of the ONE flag `uk_paging_pt_clone` takes.
// UK_PAGING_PAGE_FLAG_CLONE_NEW skips the deep copy entirely and hands back a
// table with a fresh, EMPTY top-level page (paging.c:120-121,195-206) — it
// maps NOTHING, not even the directmap. Same C function, one bit different,
// and the two results are not interchangeable: this one mints <detached!>, a
// state `pt_set_active` does not accept and never will, because nothing here
// proves it maps the code that would need to keep running after the switch.
// Named as a SEPARATE tor rather than a `new: bool` parameter on `pt_clone`
// itself so the two are a name choice, not a bit a caller can flip by mistake
// — the same DX move `unikraft/vmem` made for `UK_VMA_MAP_REPLACE`.
~pub tor pt-clone.empty { source: *PageTable }
| ok *PageTable<detached!>
| refused { errno: i32, reason: string }

~proc pt-clone.empty|zig {
    const dst = allocPt() orelse {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = -12, .reason = "out of memory allocating the destination page table" } };
    };
    const rc = uk_paging_pt_clone(dst, source, CLONE_NEW);
    if (rc != 0) {
        free(@ptrCast(dst));
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = @intCast(rc), .reason = "uk_paging_pt_clone could not allocate an empty top-level table" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = dst };
}

// ============================================================================
// PT_INIT — lifted and typed; deliberately not exercised in the boot demo
// ============================================================================
//
// See the header note "what this lift does not attempt." `<init!>` is NOT
// accepted by `pt_set_active` anywhere in this module — the fatal,
// unasserted rule this whole lift exists to name. It IS accepted by
// `pt_free`, because freeing a table that was never activated is safe on the
// C's own terms (`uk_paging_pt_free` just unmaps and frees; nothing about
// that requires the table to have ever been live).
~pub tor pt-init { start: u64, len: u64 }
| ok *PageTable<init!>
| refused { start: u64, len: u64, errno: i32, reason: string }

~proc pt-init|zig {
    if (len == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .start = start, .len = len, .errno = EINVAL, .reason = "zero-length physical range" } };
    }
    const pt = allocPt() orelse {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .start = start, .len = len, .errno = -12, .reason = "out of memory allocating the page table" } };
    };
    const rc = uk_paging_pt_init(pt, @intCast(start), @intCast(len));
    if (rc != 0) {
        free(@ptrCast(pt));
        return .{ .refused = .{ .start = start, .len = len, .errno = @intCast(rc), .reason = "uk_paging_pt_init could not set up the frame allocator or the top-level table over this range" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = pt };
}

// ============================================================================
// PT_WALK — read a page-table entry, through the directmap, no activation
// needed
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_pt_walk`, stopping at level 0 (the smallest page size this lift
// ever requests). Reachable and safe on ANY table this lift can name,
// regardless of whether it is the CPU's active one — page tables are read
// through the kernel's directmap window, which every table this lift produces
// carries a copy of (it is part of what `pt_clone` copies). This is how
// `uk_paging_init` itself populates `kernel_pt` before ever activating it
// (paging.c:1478-1545), and this lift's boot demo uses the same property to
// prove a mapping exists in a NOT-YET-ACTIVE clone before switching to it,
// without ever dereferencing the target address early.
~pub tor pt-walk { pt: *PageTable<!active-safe>, at: u64 }
| present { pt: *PageTable<active-safe!>, paddr: u64 }
| absent *PageTable<active-safe!>

~proc pt-walk|zig {
    if (!vaddrCanonical(@intCast(at))) {
        return .{ .absent = pt };
    }
    var level: c_uint = 0;
    var pte: u64 = 0;
    const rc = uk_paging_pt_walk(pt, @intCast(at), &level, null, &pte);
    if (rc != 0 or (pte & PTE_PRESENT) == 0) {
        return .{ .absent = pt };
    }
    const paddr = pte & PTE_PADDR_MASK;
    return .{ .present = .{ .pt = pt, .paddr = @intCast(paddr) } };
}

// ============================================================================
// PAGE_MAP / PAGE_UNMAP — one page at level 0, fixed read+write
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_page_mapx(pt, vaddr, UK_PAL_PADDR_INV, pages, PROT_RW, 0, NULL)`
// — physical memory allocated from `pt`'s own frame allocator (shared with
// whichever table `pt` was cloned from). Fixed to read+write and to level 0,
// the same simplification `unikraft/vmem`'s `commit` makes for its attribute
// word: the caller-mistake surface a free attribute parameter would add is
// not this lift's contribution.
//
// Takes and returns `<!active-safe>`, unchanged: mapping into a table does
// not change whether it is the table `pt_set_active` will accept — the same
// non-consuming shape `unikraft/vmem`'s `read.frozen` uses (`<!frozen>` in,
// `<frozen!>` out, identical name). This is deliberate and it is what makes
// the boot demo's ordering possible at all: map, kmap/kunmap, and pt_walk
// ALL happen while the clone is still `<active-safe!>`, i.e. still safe to
// hand to `pt_set_active` afterward. See README.md for why `page_map` is not
// offered on `<!active>` — this lift's boot demo never needs it, and adding
// it would reopen the union-collapsing question this design avoided.
~pub tor page-map { pt: *PageTable<!active-safe>, at: u64, pages: u64 }
| ok *PageTable<active-safe!>
| refused { pt: *PageTable<active-safe!>, errno: i32, reason: string }

~proc page-map|zig {
    if (pages == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = EINVAL, .reason = "zero pages" } };
    }
    if (!vaddrCanonical(@intCast(at))) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = EINVAL, .reason = "virtual address is not in canonical form" } };
    }
    const rc = uk_paging_page_mapx(pt, @intCast(at), PADDR_INV, @intCast(pages), PROT_RW, 0, null);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = @intCast(rc), .reason = "uk_paging_page_mapx could not create the mapping" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = pt };
}

~pub tor page-unmap { pt: *PageTable<!active-safe>, at: u64, pages: u64 }
| ok *PageTable<active-safe!>
| refused { pt: *PageTable<active-safe!>, errno: i32, reason: string }

~proc page-unmap|zig {
    if (pages == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = EINVAL, .reason = "zero pages" } };
    }
    const rc = uk_paging_page_unmap(pt, @intCast(at), @intCast(pages), 0);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = @intCast(rc), .reason = "uk_paging_page_unmap refused the range" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = pt };
}

// ============================================================================
// PAGE_KMAP / PAGE_KUNMAP — the clean symmetric pair the brief points at
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_page_kmap`/`_kunmap`. On x86_64 native these compile down to
// `pgarch_directmap_paddr_to_vaddr(paddr)` (arithmetic, `arch/x86_64/include/
// uk/paging/arch.h:71-77`) and a no-op respectively — the temporary mapping is
// "free" on THIS platform because the whole of physical memory is already
// directmapped. Stated honestly rather than left implied: this lift does not
// claim the pair is expensive to earn, only that the CONTRACT is real and
// general (the doc says explicitly "the number of concurrently k'mapped pages
// may be limited," which is a real constraint on platforms whose kmap is a
// small fixed window, not native's). No ordering constraint beyond pairing —
// `unikraft/vmem`'s "not everything deserves a ratchet" note about `ukalloc`
// applies here too: this is a plain acquire/release, not a multi-state
// machine, exactly the shape the brief calls out this pair for.
~pub tor page-kmap { pt: *PageTable<!active-safe>, paddr: u64, pages: u64 }
| ok { pt: *PageTable<active-safe!>, vaddr: u64 }
| refused { pt: *PageTable<active-safe!>, reason: string }

~proc page-kmap|zig {
    if (pages == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .reason = "zero pages" } };
    }
    const vaddr = uk_paging_page_kmap(pt, @intCast(paddr), @intCast(pages), 0);
    // UK_PAGING_VADDR_INV: the platform's invalid-address sentinel, aligned to
    // the largest page size — the SAME value `unikraft/vmem` derived by
    // behavioural probe and printed as 0xbaadbaad80000000
    // (unikraft/vmem/index.kz:236-241,304-312). Reused here by citation rather
    // than re-derived: it is a platform constant, not a per-library one, and
    // this lift has no behaviour to probe it with — `page_kmap` never fails on
    // this platform (see the header note), so there is no observable failure
    // to compare a guess against.
    if (vaddr == 0xbaadbaad80000000) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .reason = "uk_paging_page_kmap returned the invalid-address sentinel" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = .{ .pt = pt, .vaddr = @intCast(vaddr) } };
}

~pub tor page-kunmap { pt: *PageTable<!active-safe>, vaddr: u64, pages: u64 } -> *PageTable<active-safe!>

~proc page-kunmap|zig {
    if (pages > 0) {
        uk_paging_page_kunmap(pt, @intCast(vaddr), @intCast(pages), 0);
    }
    return pt;
}

// ============================================================================
// POKE / PEEK — NOT part of `ukpaging`'s surface
// ============================================================================
//
// A raw store/load through a virtual address, with no C call at all — the
// same shape as `unikraft/vmem`'s `write`/`read`, which do "no C call: this is
// a store through the mapping" for the identical reason. Unlike `vmem`'s pair,
// these are NOT gated by a phantom state tied to the address, because this
// module has no resource that represents "this one virtual address is
// currently mapped" — that fact lives in whichever page table is presently in
// CR3, a machine-wide, mutable fact this lift's obligations do not track (see
// the header note on the active-table singleton mismatch). `pt-walk` is how a
// caller proves an address is mapped before calling either of these; nothing
// in the TYPE SYSTEM enforces that a caller did so. Named and kept separate
// from `ukpaging`'s own tors so a reader can `grep -r "paging:poke\|paging:peek"`
// and see exactly where this lift leaves the type wall and trusts the caller.
~pub tor poke { at: u64, bytes: string }

~proc poke|zig {
    if (bytes.len == 0) return;
    const p: [*]u8 = @ptrFromInt(@as(usize, @intCast(at)));
    for (0..bytes.len) |i| p[i] = bytes[i];
}

~pub tor peek { at: u64, len: u64 } -> string

~proc peek|zig {
    const want: usize = @intCast(len);
    const p: [*]const u8 = @ptrFromInt(@as(usize, @intCast(at)));
    return p[0..want];
}

// ============================================================================
// PT_SET_ACTIVE — the switch
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_pt_set_active`: one `mov %cr3` (`arch/x86_64/.../pt.h`'s
// `uk_plat_native_pt_write_base`, an unconditional two-instruction asm block
// that always returns 0 on this platform — the `refused` arm below is real
// API surface, honestly modelled, and dead code here; see "claims this lift
// does not make"). Consumes `<!active-safe>` — reachable ONLY through
// `pt_clone` — and mints `<active!>`. This is the one call in the whole
// module that this lift cannot prove is safe by inspection; it is proven by
// the boot console reaching the print statement immediately after it. If it
// is not survivable, the console output stops here and says so.
~pub tor pt-set-active { pt: *PageTable<!active-safe> }
| ok *PageTable<active!>
| refused { pt: *PageTable<active-safe!>, errno: i32 }

~proc pt-set-active|zig {
    const rc = uk_paging_pt_set_active(pt);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = @intCast(rc) } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = pt };
}

// THE DISPOSER `<active!>` FORCES A PROGRAM TO NAME. `pt-free` never accepts
// `<!active>` (see its own header note — freeing the live table is the second
// fatal, unasserted rule this lift found). That means a program which
// activates a table and means to go on running on it forever has NO path back
// to a terminal, freed state through this lift, by design: there genuinely
// is none, on the C's own terms, short of activating a DIFFERENT table first.
// Rather than let that surface as an unexplained "obligation not discharged"
// diagnostic, this lift names the acknowledgement: a program that intends to
// keep running on the table it just activated says so here, in one grep-able
// word, the same convention as `unikraft/vmem:abandon` and
// `unikraft/blk:io.skipped`. It runs no C call — there is nothing to call;
// the table's fate is exactly what it already is; this tor only discharges
// the type-level obligation to match the runtime reality that the table
// keeps existing for as long as the kernel does.
~pub tor run-forever { pt: *PageTable<!active> }

~proc run-forever|zig {
    _ = pt;
}

// ============================================================================
// VIRT_TO_PHYS — always the ACTIVE table, by the C's own signature
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_virt_to_phys(address)` takes NO page-table parameter — it calls
// `uk_paging_pt_get_active()` internally (paging.c:1411-1430) and translates
// against whatever that returns RIGHT NOW. This lift does not fabricate a
// `pt` parameter the C does not have; the absence is the honest reflection of
// "this always queries the live table, not the one you happen to be holding."
//
// THE ASSERT THIS LIFT CANNOT RETIRE, NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. The C asserts
// `rc == 0`, `PTE_PRESENT`, and `PAGE_Lx_IS` (paging.c:1422,1424,1425) and, if
// all three are compiled out, RETURNS GARBAGE on an unmapped address — there
// is no error return path in the signature (`__paddr_t`, no sentinel). A
// caller supplying an address this lift never mapped can trip this, and this
// lift has no way to statically know every address ever mapped on the live
// table (some of it predates this program). So this tor is honestly modelled
// as always succeeding at the TYPE level — it takes a bare `u64`, not a
// phantom-typed area — and the boot demo only ever calls it with an address
// this SAME lift's own `page_map` mapped and `pt_walk` already confirmed
// present. See "claims this lift does not make."
~pub tor virt-to-phys { at: u64 } -> u64

~proc virt-to-phys|zig {
    return @intCast(uk_paging_virt_to_phys(@intCast(at)));
}

// ============================================================================
// PT_FREE — never on the table you are running on
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_paging_pt_free`. Accepts `<!init|!active-safe|!detached>` — every state
// this module can mint EXCEPT `<active!>`. See the header note: nothing in
// `uk_paging_pt_free` checks `pt != pg_active_pt`, so freeing the live table
// is a call the C will happily start and a call this lift will never make.
// `tests/negative_free_active_table.kz` is the proof.
//
// FAILURE HANDS BACK `<stuck!>`, never freed. `uk_paging_pt_free` can fail
// partway (`pg_page_unmap` returns non-zero on e.g. `-ENOMEM` during a
// required split) and, on that path, returns BEFORE reaching `pg_pt_free`
// (paging.c:328-333) — the struct is still allocated, partially unmapped, and
// this lift's storage for it must not be `free()`d underneath that state. The
// same shape as `unikraft/vmem`'s `<stuck!>`/`abandon` pair, for the same
// reason: an address range (there) or a page table (here) nobody could fully
// tear down costs the machine one leaked handle, and that is the honest price.
~pub tor pt-free { pt: *PageTable<!init|!active-safe|!detached> }
| ok
| failed { pt: *PageTable<stuck!>, errno: i32 }

~proc pt-free|zig {
    const rc = uk_paging_pt_free(pt, 0);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .failed = .{ .pt = pt, .errno = @intCast(rc) } };
    }
    free(@ptrCast(pt));
    return .{ .ok = .{} };
}

// The one way out of <stuck!>. Deliberately leaks: freeing the storage here
// would risk a double-free or a free of memory the C's own partial teardown
// left in an unknown state. Named so "we gave up on a page table" is a grep,
// not an inference — same convention as `unikraft/vmem:abandon`.
~pub tor abandon { pt: *PageTable<!stuck> }

~proc abandon|zig {
    _ = pt;
}
